    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Reynoldsburg began as Frenchtown in 1831, named after surveyor John French.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynoldsburg%2C_Ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s first post office opened in 1833 as Reynoldsburgh, changing to Reynoldsburg in 1893.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynoldsburg%2C_Ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Reynoldsburg claims to be the birthplace of the tomato, breeding the first commercial variety in the 1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynoldsburg%2C_Ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every August, the town celebrates its tomato heritage with a festival that has run nonstop since 1965.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynoldsburg%2C_Ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Blacklick Creek winds through Reynoldsburg, adding a natural waterway to the suburban landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynoldsburg%2C_Ohio"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The historic Livingston House and Museum on Graham Road, listed on the National Registry, preserves the town’s early legacy.",
